```diff
@@ -48,7 +48,9 @@
     true :boolean
     false :boolean
     42 :number
-    1/3 :number
+    ;; No ratio type on CLJS; the row splices away there (see the gated
+    ;; ratio case in number-portability below).
+    #?@(:cljs [] :default [1/3 :number])
     "s" :string
```

The `#?@` splice inside the `are` table reads fine — reader conditionals resolve at read time, before `are` expands, and `#?@` with an empty splice is already precedented in this repo (`src/cljc/yin/repl/host.cljc:15`). I used `:default` (not a `:clj`/`:cljd` pair) because `:clj` does **not** match under ClojureDart in this repo — proven by the existing `#?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable ...)` catch pattern, which would crash cljd compilation if it did. `:default` as a reader branch is also precedented (`test/dao/space/index_test.cljc:1544`). Note the file's line-137 ratio test is gated `#?(:clj ...)` only — it does not actually run on cljd; the are-table row is the only ratio-tag coverage cljd has, which is why `:default` (present on cljd) was required rather than matching that gate.

